     "<br>On Wednesday, December 4, 2019 at 2:35:13 PM UTC-5, Bader Alharbi wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><br><br></span><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font size=\"2\">Good Day Dr. Altuna,  I have 2 questions regarding the PCA function in your methyl-kit package </font></span><br><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"></span><br><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font size=\"2\">1- Regarding the function itself: can I peek to the PCA like the function summary with prcomp r function ? how I can manipulate the plots ? </font></span><br><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"></span><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font size=\"2\">I have read the documentation of PCASamples() function and I see it is based on prcomp and accept methylation percentage matrix, </font></span><br><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"></span><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"></span></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font size=\"2\">so I generated the matrix using the function mat=percMethylation() and tried to use prcomp directly but it didn&#39;t work, did I miss something?</font></span></div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><code>\r\n</code></span><br>\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0in;line-height:100%\"><br>\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:0.98in;margin-bottom:0in;font-weight:normal;line-height:100%\" align=\"left\">\r\n<font face=\"Consol\"><font style=\"font-size:11pt\" size=\"2\"></font></font></p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;margin-left:40px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">percMethylation</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">meth</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>prcomp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> scale </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>plot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prcomp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prcomp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>biplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prcomp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> scale </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div><p style=\"margin-left:1.3934in;margin-bottom:0in;font-weight:normal;line-height:100%\" align=\"left\"><font face=\"Consol\"><font style=\"font-size:11pt\" size=\"2\"><br></font></font></p><p style=\"margin-left:1.3934in;margin-bottom:0in;font-weight:normal;line-height:100%\" align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font style=\"font-size:11pt\" size=\"2\"><br></font></span></p><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><br><font size=\"2\">2<font size=\"2\">- is \r\nregarding general approach advise, I have generated the plots below, as \r\nyou can see both PCA analysis and clustering produced perfect separation\r\n between the Rat tissue types.</font></font></span></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif\"><font size=\"2\"><font size=\"2\">In the next steps, I want to isolates \r\nregions in PC1 and PC2 which contributed the most to this separation, \r\nthen I want to categorize them, tissue specific genes,  genes promoters, \r\nislands, enhancer, etc.</font></font><font size=\"2\"><font size=\"2\"> I’m guessing I need to used genmoation \r\npackage but I cannot link it to my PCA results, I only know how to annotate \r\nthe DMRs like described in the tutorial. </font></font><br>PS. my Data is single based resolution (~1.5 m CpG) from agilent sureselect targeted enriched system (not Whole genome RBSS).<br></span><br><span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\"></span><br> <p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/methylkit_discussion/attach/44e76a12902f7/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%201?part=0.1&amp;view=1&amp;authuser=0\" alt=\"\" width=\"400\" height=\"338\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/methylkit_discussion/attach/44e76a12902f7/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%202?part=0.2&amp;view=1&amp;authuser=0\" alt=\"\" width=\"400\" height=\"337\" border=\"0\"></a></p><div style=\"text-align:left\"><br></div></div><br><br> <div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><br><span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\"><font size=\"4\">my code is below </font></span><br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> <br><br> <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">#--------------loading------------ </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"col